 Recommend a round-trip test.
- The update() method has a subtle off-by-one: `for i in range(len(tokens) - n)` excludes the last valid starting index when `i + n == len(tokens) - 1 + 1`. Verify whether this is correct or whether the loop should be `range(len(tokens) - n + 1)` with the "next token" fetched defensively (currently tokens[i + n] would be out of range at the last valid start).
- Performance: the defaultdict-of-Counter structure is O(1) per insert but has high per-entry overhead. For million-token corpora, a flat array of (hash, next, count) tuples, or a compact C-level structure (e.g., numpy arrays) would dominate. Assess whether this matters for the target workload.
- Memory: the cache stores every unique n-gram. For large ngram_max (say 6) on a 1M token corpus this can balloon quickly. Suggest a top-K pruning option that keeps only the most common entries per key. This trades a small recall loss for bounded memory.
- API: there is no clear() method and no way to cap the cache size. Consider adding both, particularly if the cache is intended to be long-lived.

